        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Bob's Red Mill Protein Powder: The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Bob's Red Mill protein powder is worth buying as a plain baking and smoothie ingredient, not as a workout shake. The brand currently lists three powders on bobsredmill.com: Whey Protein Concentrate with 14 g of protein per 19 g serving, Almond Protein Powder with 20 g per 44 g, and Hemp Protein Powder with 12 g per 31 g, per the labels at the time of writing. None has added sweetener, flavor or gums, and none carries a third-party sport certification.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Buy it if</strong> you want an unsweetened, one- or two-ingredient powder to stir into oatmeal, muffins and pancakes, or you already shop the brand at your grocery store. <strong>Skip it if</strong> you want 25 g or more of protein in one scoop, a flavored shake, or a powder with NSF or Informed Choice testing. For those goals, our <a href="/best-protein-powder/">best protein powder</a> guide is the better starting point.
          </p>

