            2. Bob's Red Mill Protein Powders and the Best Alternatives
          </h2>

          <p>
            The three powders Bob's Red Mill still lists on its website, plus two single-ingredient alternatives on Amazon: a third-party-certified whey and a pea protein to replace the Bob's Red Mill pea powder that is no longer sold. Prices are the Amazon prices we saw on 17 November 2026 and will change.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="whey" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Is in Bob's Red Mill Whey Protein Powder?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Bob's Red Mill whey protein powder contains two ingredients, whey protein concentrate and sunflower lecithin, and gives 14 g of protein in a 1/4 cup (19 g) serving, per the label at the time of writing. That serving has 70 calories, 1 g of fat, 3 g of carbohydrate, 1 g of total sugar with 0 g added, 45 mg of sodium and 64 mg of calcium. There is no sweetener, no flavoring, no thickener and no added vitamins. The sunflower lecithin is there to help the powder wet and disperse in liquid, which is why the brand says it dissolves instantly.
          </p>

          <p>
            The number that matters most is protein density. Fourteen grams of protein in 19 g of powder works out to about 74% protein by weight, which is typical for an unflavored whey concentrate and much higher than the almond or hemp powders. The catch is the scoop size. Most sports wheys use a 30 to 33 g scoop that delivers 24 to 25 g of protein. To get 25 g from Bob's Red Mill whey you need about 1.8 servings, roughly 34 g of powder and about 125 calories. That is not a problem in a recipe, where you measure by the cup anyway, but it is a surprise if you expect one scoop to make a full shake.
          </p>

          <p>
            The bag is labelled whey protein concentrate rather than isolate, and that distinction matters for anyone sensitive to dairy. Concentrate keeps more of the milk sugar than isolate, so it is not lactose free, and it is a milk allergen. Bob's Red Mill does not publish a lactose figure. If lactose bothers you, an isolate is the safer choice; our <a href="/whey-vs-whey-isolate/">whey vs whey isolate</a> explainer covers the difference, and the <a href="/best-lactose-free-protein-powder/">best lactose-free protein powder</a> guide lists options.
          </p>

          <p>
            Bob's Red Mill does not say where its whey is sourced, whether the cows are grass fed, or whether it is made from rBST-free milk on the product page we checked. It also does not publish an amino acid profile beyond saying whey is a complete protein containing all of the essential amino acids, which is true of whey in general. The bag now comes in a stand-up, resealable package, per the brand, and Amazon sells it mostly as 12 oz bags in packs of two or four.
          </p>

          <p>
            In short, this is a simple, honest whey with a short ingredient list and modest scoops. People searching for Bob's Red Mill whey protein powder usually want exactly that: a plain dairy protein for cooking that will not make muffins taste like vanilla frosting. If you want it to work as a gym shake, plan on two scoops and some fruit or cocoa for flavor.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="almond-hemp" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Is in Bob's Red Mill Almond and Hemp Protein Powder?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Bob's Red Mill almond protein powder is one ingredient, defatted blanched almonds, and gives 20 g of protein per 1/3 cup (44 g); the hemp protein powder is one ingredient, hemp protein, and gives 12 g per 1/4 cup (31 g), per the labels at the time of writing. Both are whole-food style powders rather than isolates, which means they carry fiber, minerals and some fat alongside the protein. That makes them nutritious ingredients but less efficient protein sources than whey.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Almond Protein Powder label facts</h3>

          <p>
            The almond powder is made by grinding blanched almonds and expeller pressing them to remove the oil, without chemical solvents, per Bob's Red Mill. A 44 g serving has 180 calories, 5 g of fat, 14 g of carbohydrate, 6 g of fiber, 4 g of naturally occurring sugar with 0 g added, 246 mg of calcium (20% of the Daily Value), 3 mg of iron and 601 mg of potassium. Protein makes up about 45% of the powder by weight. The product page carries badges for gluten free, vegan, Non-GMO, paleo friendly and Kosher pareve. It is a tree nut product, so anyone with an almond allergy should avoid it. We compare it with other nut-based powders in our <a href="/best-almond-protein-powder/">best almond protein powder</a> guide.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Hemp Protein Powder label facts</h3>

          <p>
            A 31 g serving of the hemp powder has 120 calories, 2 g of fat, 13 g of carbohydrate, 11 g of fiber, 1 g of sugar, 6 mg of iron (35% of the Daily Value) and 391 mg of potassium, per the label. Only about 39% of the powder is protein, so getting 25 g takes roughly 65 g of powder, about 250 calories and 23 g of fiber. That much fiber in one sitting can cause bloating if you are not used to it. Bob's Red Mill calls hemp a complete protein containing all of the essential amino acids; hemp is generally lower in lysine than animal proteins, which is one reason plant blends often pair it with pea. Our <a href="/best-hemp-protein-powder/">best hemp protein powder</a> page compares it with organic and higher-protein hemp options.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What happened to Bob's Red Mill pea protein?</h3>

          <p>
            Bob's Red Mill pea protein is no longer listed on bobsredmill.com, and we found no current Bob's Red Mill pea protein listing on Amazon when we checked on 17 November 2026. The same goes for the older soy protein isolate and the flavored Protein Powder Nutritional Boosters: none appears in the brand's current product catalogue. Treat them as discontinued, and be wary of third-party sellers offering old stock at inflated prices. Our <a href="/best-pea-protein-powder/">best pea protein powder</a> and <a href="/best-soy-protein-powder/">best soy protein powder</a> guides list current single-ingredient replacements.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="third-party-tested" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Is Bob's Red Mill Protein Powder Third-Party Tested?
          </h2>

          <p>
            No, Bob's Red Mill protein powder is not third-party tested for sport or contaminants in the way supplement buyers usually mean. None of the three powders lists NSF Certified for Sport, NSF Contents Certified, Informed Choice, Informed Sport or Clean Label Project status on its product page, and Bob's Red Mill does not publish heavy metal results for them. That is not unusual for a grocery brand that sells protein powder as a baking ingredient, but it matters if you are a tested athlete or you specifically want contaminant data.
          </p>

          <p>
            What Bob's Red Mill does have is food-safety certification at the facility level. The company says it is audited every year against the Safe Quality Food (SQF) standard, a GFSI-benchmarked food safety program, and that it runs HACCP and Good Manufacturing Practice programs. It also says all of its products are certified kosher by OK Kosher and KOF-K, that more than 240 products are Non-GMO Project Verified, and that its organic products are certified by Quality Assurance International. Those certify how food is made and labelled. They do not test a finished protein powder for banned substances or verify its protein content.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Is Bob's Red Mill protein powder gluten free?</h3>

          <p>
            Only the almond protein powder carries a gluten-free badge on Bob's Red Mill's website at the time of writing. The brand runs a separate 58,200 square foot gluten-free facility and says it batch tests gluten-free products in its own laboratory with an ELISA gluten assay, with some products also certified by the Gluten-Free Certification Organization. The hemp protein page now states that the product is no longer produced gluten free, and the whey page shows only a Kosher dairy badge. If you have celiac disease, buy the almond powder or check that the bag you pick up carries the red GF logo and testing statement.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Food-safety audits are not supplement testing</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">SQF, kosher and Non-GMO marks tell you how a factory is run. Banned-substance and heavy-metal testing comes from programs such as NSF and Informed Choice. Bob's Red Mill has the first kind, not the second. If you need the second, see our guide to protein powder without heavy metals.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            If testing is the deciding factor, the swap is simple. Naked Whey and Naked Pea are similar single-ingredient powders that Naked says are NSF Contents Certified, and our <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-heavy-metals/">protein powder without heavy metals</a> guide covers more options. For a broader look at the risks, read <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">is protein powder safe</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

